adjudication.rationale:
The handler computes `uniqueGuests` by filtering the input `guests` only against the *existing* `booking.attendees` and a blacklist:

```ts
const uniqueGuests = guests.filter(
  (guest) => !booking.attendees.some((attendee) => guest === attendee.email) && ...
);
```

This does **not** deduplicate within the `guests` array itself (e.g., `['a@x.com','a@x.com']` will keep both entries if `a@x.com` isn’t already an attendee). Those values are then passed directly to `attendees.createMany`, which can insert duplicate rows unless the DB layer prevents it.

Also, because the check is based on a snapshot read before the `update`, two concurrent calls can both see the same pre-update attendee set and both attempt to insert the same guest emails.

The part about there being *no* `UNIQUE(bookingId,email)` constraint is not verifiable from this diff, but the missing in-batch dedup and the race window are real issues in the shown code.

In addGuests.handler.ts the permission flag is computed as:

const isTeamAdminOrOwner = (await isTeamAdmin(...)) && (await isTeamOwner(...));

Despite the variable name implying an OR, the code requires both checks to be true. This will exclude team ADMINs (and potentially everyone if the predicates are mutually exclusive) from passing the team-role permission gate, which is a concrete logic/authorization defect introduced in this diff.
